%description:

Tcp.adaptiveDelayedAcks:rfc -- the delayed ACK of RFC 9293 waits also at the start of a connection.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "false: the delayed ACK of RFC 9293 section 3.8.6.3 (delayedAckTimeout,
delayedAckFrameCount)".

The classes: rfc (false, the default), and three facets of true (LinuxTcp): linux-quickack,
linux-ato and linux-pingpong. This test separates "rfc" from the facets: with true, the
quickack mode acknowledges the first segments of a connection at once.

Host under test: host2, the receiver. Peer: host1 sends one segment of 100 bytes, the first
data of the connection. Observation: level 1, the ACK of host2: none in the first 100 ms
after the arrival, one before 0.5 s.

%# parameter: Tcp.adaptiveDelayedAcks
%# class: rfc
%# value: false
%# key: *.host2.tcp.adaptiveDelayedAcks
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpAdaptiveDelayedAcksRfc")
        .once(on("host2.tcp").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == false && pk.dataLength > tcp.headerLength")
                  .describe("the segment of 100 bytes at host2")
                  .within(0.5))
        .never(on("host2.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                   .filterPacket("tcp.ackBit == true")
                   .describe("an ACK of host2 in the first 100 ms")
                   .within(0.1))
        .once(on("host2.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.ackBit == true")
                  .describe("the delayed ACK of host2")
                  .within(0.4));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpAdaptiveDelayedAcksRfc compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host2.tcp.adaptiveDelayedAcks = false    # the parameter under test
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 100B
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpAdaptiveDelayedAcksRfc: PASS
